<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><channel><language>en</language><title>Breaking news from AFRICA, Maghreb and Sub-Saharan Africa - France 24</title><description>FRANCE 24’s coverage of events, politics and social issues across Africa, including North Africa (the Maghreb) and the Sahel, aided by our network of correspondents and experts across the continent.</description><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/</link><lastBuildDate>Sat, 28 Nov 2026 06:26:09 GMT</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/rss" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Uganda to withdraw troops from DR Congo</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20261127-uganda-to-withdraw-troops-from-dr-congo</link><description>
First, Uganda is heading for the exit in eastern DR Congo. Kampala has announced the withdrawal of its troops from a key town in North Kivu, as relations with Kinshasa deteriorate following provocative comments from Uganda's army chief. Then, pregnant women are struggling to cope with extreme heat, while health experts warn of growing risks for unborn babies, from dehydration to premature births. And, after seeking safety, opportunity and a better life, many migrants in South Africa now face hostility, violence and growing exclusion. As anti-immigration sentiment gains ground, the country's promise is fading for those who once saw it as a land of hope.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/92a9d6c2-eaa6-11f1-8c67-1991d4929b54/w:1024/p:16x9/DL00006224-VOD-00023676-01.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/92a9d6c2-eaa6-11f1-8c67-1991d4929b54/w:1024/p:16x9/DL00006224-VOD-00023676-01.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">2f58a35a-eaab-11f1-873d-95f9105a3c8e</guid><pubDate>Fri, 27 Nov 2026 21:44:29 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clarisse FORTUNÉ</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Uganda to withdraw troops from DR Congo town after army chief's threat sparks row</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261127-uganda-withdraw-troops-dr-congo-town-after-army-chief-threat-row</link><description>
Uganda's army on Friday said it planned to withdraw from the eastern DR Congo town of Lubero in North Kivu. The announcement came days after Ugandan army chief Muhoozi Kainerugaba – son of President Yoweri Museveni – threatened to take control of Walikale, another North Kivu town, sparking a diplomatic spat between the two neighbouring nations.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c2217142-ea80-11f1-be7e-77d069279b82/w:1024/p:16x9/AFP-20241220-36QY39P-v1-HighRes-DrcongoRwandaColnflictDisplaced.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c2217142-ea80-11f1-be7e-77d069279b82/w:1024/p:16x9/AFP-20241220-36QY39P-v1-HighRes-DrcongoRwandaColnflictDisplaced.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">ea8c3c02-ea80-11f1-ab06-3d26d2c0fc49</guid><pubDate>Fri, 27 Nov 2026 17:56:28 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c2217142-ea80-11f1-be7e-77d069279b82/w:1024/p:16x9/AFP-20241220-36QY39P-v1-HighRes-DrcongoRwandaColnflictDisplaced.jpg">© Philemon Barbier, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Reporters</category><title>Immigration: South Africa no longer an El Dorado for foreigners</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/reporters/20261127-immigration-south-africa-no-longer-an-el-dorado-for-foreigners</link><description>
South Africa was once a welcoming country; a refuge for those fleeing conflict and persecution. But for thousands of foreigners, the dream of a new life in South Africa is turning into more of a nightmare. Against a backdrop of unemployment and economic hardship, anti-immigration movements are gaining ground. Our correspondents report.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/defb5750-e9b4-11f1-8375-f9cf020caef6/w:1024/p:16x9/Reporters-FR-EN-261126-v2.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/defb5750-e9b4-11f1-8375-f9cf020caef6/w:1024/p:16x9/Reporters-FR-EN-261126-v2.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">86290ab0-e9b3-11f1-bcbd-dd00a2c5f741</guid><pubDate>Fri, 27 Nov 2026 11:02:19 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Caroline DUMAY</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Nigeria faces rising election violence in run-up to vote</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/nigeria-faces-rising-election-violence-in-run-up-to-vote</link><description>
In tonight's edition: Concerns are mounting that electoral violence could deter voters from taking part in Nigeria's presidential election in January. Also, the driver of a car suspected of causing a fuel tanker to overturn on a major highway in Egypt is detained. Plus Sierra Leone was once considered the most dangerous place in the world to give birth. Today, maternal deaths have dropped dramatically.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2604f4bc-ea55-11f1-94ea-1991d4929b54/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-2a74733e.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2604f4bc-ea55-11f1-94ea-1991d4929b54/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-2a74733e.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">92e6ded2-e9de-11f1-813e-59c451a64275</guid><pubDate>Thu, 26 Nov 2026 21:21:46 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Georja CALVIN-SMITH</dc:creator></item><item><category>Across Africa</category><title>Across Africa: Tunisian photographer's family focus</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/across-africa-tunisian-photographer-s-family-focus-1</link><description>
We bring you the stories from the continent with Georja Calvin-Smith. This week, health workers struggling to keep pace with DR Congo's Ebola outbreak say they feel abandoned and unsupported. Also, this summer's World Cup was about more than winning – for some fans, at least. Tunisia's performance may not have been all that its supporters had hoped, but sports photographer Hosni Manoubi is far from negative about a family tradition of documenting his country's international sports showings. [Archive episode from June 2026].
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d42e595e-e9b3-11f1-9008-1991d4929b54/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-ebcee408.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d42e595e-e9b3-11f1-9008-1991d4929b54/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-ebcee408.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">0134df40-70a1-11f1-a2d1-bbc19ff962e2</guid><pubDate>Thu, 26 Nov 2026 16:19:12 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Georja CALVIN-SMITH</dc:creator></item><item><category>Across Africa</category><title>Across Africa: The scars of South Africa's Soweto uprising</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/across-africa-the-scars-of-south-africa-s-soweto-uprising-1</link><description>
Fifty years ago, thousands of young South Africans from Soweto protested against apartheid's stranglehold on their education and the imposition of Afrikaans as a language of instruction. What began as a peaceful protest ended in bloodshed. Also, men in northern Benin confront uncomfortable realities about the lives of the women who live with and love them. [Archive episode from June 2026].
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c1d4d7f8-e9ac-11f1-9223-1991d4929b54/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-d4bd2670.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c1d4d7f8-e9ac-11f1-9223-1991d4929b54/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-d4bd2670.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">6307a380-6b22-11f1-b299-cb59d2b33f34</guid><pubDate>Thu, 26 Nov 2026 15:28:19 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Georja CALVIN-SMITH</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Egypt: 31 people killed in Cairo after fuel truck overturns</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261126-egypt-31-people-killed-in-cairo-after-fuel-truck-overturns</link><description>
A fire broke out on a busy highway in Egypt’s capital, Cairo, on Wednesday, killing at least 31 people after a fuel truck reportedly overturned. The suspected driver was arrested on Thursday, according to Egypt’s public prosecutor’s office.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/caddf70c-e98a-11f1-ad2a-77d069279b82/w:1024/p:16x9/EGYPT-Thumbnail.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/caddf70c-e98a-11f1-ad2a-77d069279b82/w:1024/p:16x9/EGYPT-Thumbnail.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e3adaf34-e98a-11f1-9d77-8399c326a9ea</guid><pubDate>Thu, 26 Nov 2026 11:28:29 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>'We are not fighting culture, we fight to end FGM: It's a human rights violation of women and girls'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/we-are-not-fighting-culture-we-fight-to-end-fgm-it-s-a-human-rights-violation-of-women-and-girls</link><description>
“We fight to end FGM because it is a human rights violation of women and girls. We are not fighting culture.” Caroline Lagat, Legal Advisor at Equality Now’s Africa office in Nairobi, discusses the challenges of ending female genital mutilation (FGM) and child marriage. She points to insufficient funding, conflicting marriage laws and growing community resistance to campaigns against FGM. While acknowledging some progress, Lagat calls on the government to be "the face of the campaign to end FGM and child marriage.”
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2a34f374-e970-11f1-a19b-174dac1c1bf0/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261125-080511-081235-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2a34f374-e970-11f1-a19b-174dac1c1bf0/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261125-080511-081235-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">2fb6ce30-e970-11f1-a995-43c632b8dd93</guid><pubDate>Thu, 26 Nov 2026 08:39:26 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>At least 31 killed in Cairo fire after fuel truck overturns on busy highway</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261126-at-least-31-killed-in-cairo-fire-after-fuel-truck-overturns-on-busy-highway</link><description>
At least 31 people were killed and 15 injured when a fuel truck overturned and burst into flames on a busy highway in Cairo on Wednesday, engulfing nearby vehicles, Egyptian authorities said.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ac48570e-e937-11f1-b861-1991d4929b54/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26329710858344.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ac48570e-e937-11f1-b861-1991d4929b54/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26329710858344.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">5391f010-e938-11f1-b846-df9d5930b598</guid><pubDate>Thu, 26 Nov 2026 01:25:54 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/ac48570e-e937-11f1-b861-1991d4929b54/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26329710858344.jpg">© Mohammed Salah, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Cape Town court shuts down Ramaphosa's Farmgate scandal</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/cape-town-court-shuts-down-ramaphosa-s-farmgate-scandal</link><description>
In tonight's edition: South Africa's president sidesteps an impeachment bid by the opposition. Also, we hear from some of the Malagasy women fighting for an overhaul of reproductive rights in their country. And in eastern DR Congo, the CRP rebel group halts its advance after coming within dozens of kilometres of the northeastern city of Bunia.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/25db30b6-e981-11f1-93d4-81bc2b1dcd65/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-97cad935.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/25db30b6-e981-11f1-93d4-81bc2b1dcd65/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-97cad935.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">47ee50ca-e918-11f1-abf2-7ff6950b734e</guid><pubDate>Wed, 25 Nov 2026 21:41:01 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Georja CALVIN-SMITH</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Senegal reinstated as AFCON 2025 winners</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/senegal-reinstated-as-afcon-2025-winners</link><description>
In tonight's edition: Senegal are reinstated as 2025 Africa Cup of Nations winners after sport's highest court upheld their appeal against being stripped of the title. We bring you reactions and analysis from our Sports Journalist James Vasina.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d8a120d4-e84b-11f1-8842-29d00e23de9a/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-3a2ff429.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d8a120d4-e84b-11f1-8842-29d00e23de9a/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-3a2ff429.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">a595a2d2-e84b-11f1-bf88-d159e8ae7f25</guid><pubDate>Tue, 24 Nov 2026 21:14:42 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clarisse FORTUNÉ</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Senegal reinstated as 2025 Africa Cup of Nations winners</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261124-senegal-reinstated-as-2025-africa-cup-of-nations-winners</link><description>
Senegal ​were reinstated as 2025 Africa Cup of Nations winners ​on Tuesday after the Court of Arbitration for Sport upheld their appeal against being stripped of the title. Senegal contested the ​Confederation ‌of African Football's decision to declare ⁠Morocco champions, despite Senegal beating the hosts 1-0 after extra time in ‌January's final in Rabat. Morocco announced that it would “pursue all necessary legal procedures” in response to the decision.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/cca0443c-e81d-11f1-8930-c131ae9bfe9c/w:1024/p:16x9/MINI-AFCON.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/cca0443c-e81d-11f1-8930-c131ae9bfe9c/w:1024/p:16x9/MINI-AFCON.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e5654f3a-e81d-11f1-a824-7dda7bd44321</guid><pubDate>Tue, 24 Nov 2026 16:16:03 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Focus</category><title>Rwanda's malaria cases on the rise: Climate change leads to more mosquitoes</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261124-rwanda-s-malaria-cases-on-the-rise-climate-change-leads-to-more-mosquitoes</link><description>
In Rwanda, malaria cases are on the rise due to climate change. Higher temperatures and prolonged rainfall are creating more breeding grounds for mosquitoes. According to researchers, the risk of infection could increase by as much as 20 percent by 2100 in the Rift Valley. Our correspondent Aurore Bayoud reports on Rwanda's efforts to tackle the problem.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c9c06476-e7e2-11f1-adb6-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-01df9741.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c9c06476-e7e2-11f1-adb6-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-01df9741.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">3bab74a8-e75d-11f1-86fa-4579bb4eb38e</guid><pubDate>Tue, 24 Nov 2026 13:52:59 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Aurore Bayoud</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Africa disappointed with COP31 outcome</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa-disappointed-with-cop31-outcome</link><description>
In tonight's edition: The COP31 climate summit in Turkey has failed to reach an agreement, exposing deep divisions over fossil fuels and climate finance. Also, DR Congo's Félix Tshisekedi and Kenya's William Ruto agree to restore relations and boost regional cooperation. And determined to help their children, parents in a South African township have transformed a discarded shipping container into a sensory room.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/85103132-e7ef-11f1-9df6-81da3ab2acbb/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-33e2407e.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/85103132-e7ef-11f1-9df6-81da3ab2acbb/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-33e2407e.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">d9faa548-e78a-11f1-9d0f-859f8c655867</guid><pubDate>Mon, 23 Nov 2026 22:14:17 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clarisse FORTUNÉ</dc:creator></item><item><category>The Debate</category><title>How far will Ukraine war spread? Kyiv targets Russian vessels in Libya</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261123-how-far-will-ukraine-war-spread-kyiv-targets-russian-vessels-in-libya</link><description>
Talk about a spillover effect. In the Libyan port of Benghazi, Ukraine is claiming responsibility for the sinkings first of a Russian warship and then a cargo vessel. That's on top of the sanctioned ship struck in the Baltic Sea that's since strayed into Finland's waters. Are these legitimate targets?
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/dd313f6c-e76f-11f1-85b8-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-23T102302Z-464352545-RC2MTOAS4A1W-RTRMADP-3-UKRAINE-CRISIS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/dd313f6c-e76f-11f1-85b8-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-23T102302Z-464352545-RC2MTOAS4A1W-RTRMADP-3-UKRAINE-CRISIS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">b1974dca-e76e-11f1-8a31-e3a0f7f6a4a0</guid><pubDate>Mon, 23 Nov 2026 19:41:45 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>François PICARD</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Ukraine claims strikes on Russian ships off Libyan coast</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261123-ukraine-claims-strikes-on-russian-ships-off-libyan-coast</link><description>
Ukraine claimed responsibility on Monday for attacks on two Russian vessels off the coast of Libya the previous day. Here is what we know about the operation, claimed by President Volodymyr Zelensky shortly after the United Nations called for an investigation into the strikes.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b333898c-e746-11f1-aae4-6b451f569414/w:1024/p:16x9/miniukraine-1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b333898c-e746-11f1-aae4-6b451f569414/w:1024/p:16x9/miniukraine-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">b99b296a-e746-11f1-907d-f78eb5920847</guid><pubDate>Mon, 23 Nov 2026 14:36:26 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Russian warship hit by Ukrainian drone at Benghazi naval base, Libyan officials say</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261122-russian-warship-hit-by-suspected-ukrainian-drone-at-libya-s-benghazi-naval-base</link><description>
A Russian navy corvette docked at the Benghazi naval base ​in eastern Libya was hit on Sunday by a Ukrainian sea drone, according to local officials, hours before a cargo ship was also hit by a sea drone in a second attack. Ukraine's President Volodymyr Zelensky on Monday confirmed that his country's military was behind the strikes.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/659e3cc8-e6a9-11f1-ae55-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26326649718247.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/659e3cc8-e6a9-11f1-ae55-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26326649718247.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">59c27282-e6a1-11f1-8d36-99870da4d23e</guid><pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 19:41:23 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/659e3cc8-e6a9-11f1-ae55-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26326649718247.jpg">© AP via Vanto satellite image</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Drone strikes hit homes and UN building in Sudan's Khartoum</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261122-drone-strikes-hit-homes-and-un-building-in-sudan-s-khartoum</link><description>
Drone strikes damaged residential homes and a United Nations building in Sudan's capital Khartoum early Sunday, with the army accusing neighbouring Ethiopia of launching the attacks. The strikes came as drone warfare intensifies in the country's conflict, now in its fourth year. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1c5eb51c-e693-11f1-9b46-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/5b43f69bec605872bb956153617567218ceba021.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1c5eb51c-e693-11f1-9b46-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/5b43f69bec605872bb956153617567218ceba021.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1d39bae0-e693-11f1-a9ff-1deb7bcd9931</guid><pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 16:38:14 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1c5eb51c-e693-11f1-9b46-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/5b43f69bec605872bb956153617567218ceba021.jpg">© Ebrahim Hamid, AFP </source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Ethiopia: Tackling period poverty and stigma</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/ethiopia-tackling-period-poverty-and-stigma</link><description><![CDATA[
In Ethiopia, entrepreneur Hanan Ahmed is tackling period poverty and the stigma surrounding menstruation. By producing affordable reusable sanitary pads and raising awareness in schools, she hopes to make menstrual health more accessible and help girls stay in class. Report by Awa Diouf & Guillaume Gougeon.
]]></description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2a9827a2-e5a4-11f1-8650-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261120-215007-215136-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2a9827a2-e5a4-11f1-8650-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261120-215007-215136-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">2f6b1c62-e5a4-11f1-9016-8169673db442</guid><pubDate>Sat, 21 Nov 2026 12:22:44 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Guinea bans Burkina Faso broadcaster, escalating diplomatic tensions</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20261120-guinea-bans-burkina-faso-broadcaster-escalating-diplomatic-tensions</link><description>
Tensions rise between Guinea and Burkina Faso after Conakry bans Burkinabé broadcaster RTB, amid a diplomatic dispute over gold miners and a caricature of President Mamadi Doumbouya. Meanwhile, fighting erupts between Congolese forces and the Rwanda-backed FRN group in eastern DR Congo, despite peace commitments. Finally, in Ethiopia, one entrepreneur is tackling period poverty by breaking menstrual stigma and making reusable sanitary pads more accessible.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/8e31d6f6-e52b-11f1-9535-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-a6bd8a5c.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/8e31d6f6-e52b-11f1-9535-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-a6bd8a5c.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4a97e566-e52b-11f1-9c5f-9fcf7db39ba3</guid><pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 21:53:35 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clémence WALLER</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Kenyan marathon champion Peres Jepchirchir banned for doping</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20261119-kenyan-marathon-champion-banned-for-doping</link><description>
In tonight's edition: Kenyan athletics superstar and reigning marathon world champion Peres Jepchirchir is banned for three years for doping. Also, South African judges leave the courtroom behind and tour a repatriation centre before handing down judgment in 33 emergency asylum applications. Plus Ghana's presidency has a new jet, but critics of the ruling party are not on board. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/42bd4652-e4c1-11f1-ac8d-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-f05f83d7.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/42bd4652-e4c1-11f1-ac8d-13439ca2fe89/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-f05f83d7.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">dec457cc-e460-11f1-83ea-db85787c2904</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 21:38:55 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Georja CALVIN-SMITH</dc:creator></item><item><category>Tête à tête</category><title>US has 'unbalanced approach' on DR Congo deal, Rwanda's foreign minister says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t%C3%AAte-%C3%A0-t%C3%AAte/20261119-us-has-unbalanced-approach-on-dr-congo-deal-rwanda-s-foreign-minister-says</link><description>
In an interview with FRANCE 24, Rwandan Foreign Minister Olivier Nduhungirehe said the Trump administration had "an unbalanced approach" in implementing the DR Congo peace deal it brokered. He also said that the recent row over the Organisation Internationale de la Francophonie's top job was "manufactured in Paris and Kinshasa". Finally, he defended a deal for Rwanda to host failed asylum seekers from five European countries.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1da1c348-e417-11f1-91e2-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/FR-NW-PKG-CLEAN-EN-TETE-A-TETE-Olivier-J-P-Nduhungirehe-AUBIN-Sophian.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1da1c348-e417-11f1-91e2-1bac47ab2f5d/w:1024/p:16x9/FR-NW-PKG-CLEAN-EN-TETE-A-TETE-Olivier-J-P-Nduhungirehe-AUBIN-Sophian.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">3e342fd8-e42b-11f1-8816-c724df93fed8</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 17:07:09 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Marc PERELMAN</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Nigeria: States set to get their own police forces</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/nigeria-states-set-to-get-their-own-police-forces</link><description>
Nigeria has moved a step closer to letting its 36 states set up their own police forces. The National Assembly has sent a constitutional amendment bill to the president, as authorities seek state-run police to complement the federal force amid growing insecurity. Many states currently fund vigilante groups to fight armed groups like Boko Haram, but these groups are poorly trained and ill-equipped for the task. FRANCE 24's correspondent has the latest.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/85e5a052-e410-11f1-8151-6b451f569414/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261118-214527-214725-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/85e5a052-e410-11f1-8151-6b451f569414/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261118-214527-214725-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8aa44116-e410-11f1-995e-c3a586aca863</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:20:35 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Senegal: Pastef removes President Faye from its ranks</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/senegal-pastef-removes-president-faye-from-its-ranks</link><description>
In Senegal, Pastef, the majority party led by National Assembly president Ousmane Sonko, has made official the departure of President Bassirou Diomaye Faye. It is the latest episode in a split with his former Prime minister that was already largely complete, and it comes in the middle of an economic crisis.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a851556a-e40f-11f1-b1b5-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261118-214326-214524-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a851556a-e40f-11f1-b1b5-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261118-214326-214524-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">ad72f0d0-e40f-11f1-aee3-d1fc0af3cb61</guid><pubDate>Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:05:40 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>President Bassirou Diomaye Faye excluded from Senegal's ruling party</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/bassirou-diomaye-faye-excluded-from-senegal-s-ruling-party</link><description>
In tonight's edition: The political divorce between Senegal's president and his powerful former prime minister and mentor is official. Also, all of Nigeria's states have shown willingness for a push to create their own police forces. Plus we see how in parts of the continent, conflict and a drop in funding have placed huge pressure on children's learning. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a145d098-e41e-11f1-9507-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-a317f3ba.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a145d098-e41e-11f1-9507-e56ab3dc0809/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-a317f3ba.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">10fc3a7e-e395-11f1-8fb8-2356d0896181</guid><pubDate>Wed, 18 Nov 2026 21:21:28 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Georja CALVIN-SMITH</dc:creator></item><item><category>Focus</category><title>Chinese cars shake up Ivory Coast's auto industry</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261118-chinese-cars-shake-up-ivory-coast-s-auto-industry</link><description>
In the space of just a few years, more than a dozen Chinese carmakers have established themselves in Ivory Coast and are becoming increasingly visible on the country's streets. How have they managed to win over a growing number of customers? FRANCE 24's Jean Barrère and Eunice Stolz-Masson report.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b764e906-e34a-11f1-82d5-ff16da94d1fe/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-9e1e00a0.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b764e906-e34a-11f1-82d5-ff16da94d1fe/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-9e1e00a0.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">68e7eeb0-e2ad-11f1-917c-0b2e0a4e23f7</guid><pubDate>Wed, 18 Nov 2026 12:25:36 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Jean BARRERE</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>AI imagery complicates Nigeria's election preparations</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/ai-imagery-complicates-nigeria-s-election-prep</link><description>
In tonight's edition: Concerns emerge that artificial intelligence has been used to generate fake voting profiles ahead of Nigeria's high-stakes general election in January. Also, Nigeria's mining marshal heads underground to unearth some of the people and organisations behind the vast illegal mining industries. Plus a groundbreaking archaeological discovery in northern Sudan could be the ancient site of the largest church ever built in one of medieval Nubia's most important Christian sites. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/65b601b6-e32d-11f1-b8b0-c5c0357b2a0c/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-82f8fe31.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/65b601b6-e32d-11f1-b8b0-c5c0357b2a0c/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-82f8fe31.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4e7b18ee-e2cc-11f1-a02c-ff1458ed0a1b</guid><pubDate>Tue, 17 Nov 2026 21:20:27 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Georja CALVIN-SMITH</dc:creator></item><item><category>Spotlight</category><title>Predictable: DR Congo-Rwanda rivalry spills over to Francophonie summit</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261116-la-francophonie-waning-in-global-influence-linguistic-institutions-becoming-less-important</link><description>
François Picard is pleased to welcome Phil Clark, professor of international politics at SOAS University of London. The diplomatic crisis between Rwanda and DR Congo spilling out at the Francophonie summit in Phnom Penh has exposed an unexpected shift in their relations. Clark says that "nobody expected Rwanda to back down in this way" following President Emmanuel Macron's request that President Paul Kagame withdraw Louise Mushikiwabo's candidacy for a third term as head of the group.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/6832fb3c-e298-11f1-bd8e-ff16da94d1fe/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-2cf9efa9.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/6832fb3c-e298-11f1-bd8e-ff16da94d1fe/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-2cf9efa9.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">3a0c84c0-e20d-11f1-913b-771c1de741c0</guid><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 22:29:45 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>François PICARD</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Rwanda to host hub for rejected EU migrants</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/rwanda-to-host-hub-for-rejected-eu-migrants</link><description>
In tonight's edition: Five European countries are negotiating a deal to deport rejected asylum seekers to Rwanda. Also, DR Congo and Rwanda's political animosity spills over at the Francophonie Summit in Cambodia. Plus in Tunisia, young women are increasingly turning towards weightlifting and bodybuilding as a form of self-expression.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b210f7d0-e27b-11f1-83ae-c5c0357b2a0c/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-4250847b.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b210f7d0-e27b-11f1-83ae-c5c0357b2a0c/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-4250847b.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4854abbc-e202-11f1-a03e-fdd6321e2ad6</guid><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 21:21:14 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clémence WALLER</dc:creator></item><item><category>Africa</category><title>Francophonie summit postpones new leader vote amid DR Congo-Rwanda dispute</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20261116-francophonie-summit-postpones-new-leader-vote-amid-dr-congo-rwanda-dispute</link><description>
The Organisation Internationale de la Francophonie (OIF) on Monday had been set to choose its new secretary general during talks in the Cambodian capital Phnom Penh. But delegates decided to postpone the election for a year after a walkout threat by the Democratic Republic of Congo amid an ongoing dispute between Kinshasa and Kigali.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3df16a22-e188-11f1-be7e-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D7HJ4YA.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3df16a22-e188-11f1-be7e-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D7HJ4YA.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8b068e9a-e1a7-11f1-a0c0-95c7966141b5</guid><pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 10:51:40 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3df16a22-e188-11f1-be7e-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D7HJ4YA.jpg">© Tang Chin Sothy, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item></channel></rss>
